  <static seed="78" source="Library/WHFreeman/Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition/15_Multiple_Integration/15.2_Double_Integrals_over_More_General_Regions/15.2.7.pg">
    <statement><p>Compute the double integral of   <m>f(x, y) = 50 x^{4} y</m>  over the given shaded domain in the following Figure :</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__15_Multiple_Integration__15__2_Double_Integrals_over_More_General_Regions__15__2__7__pg-image-1.png" width="22%"/>
<p><m>\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{D}} 50 x^{4} y \,dA =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>20297.1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>0\le y\le 2,\quad y\le x\le 4</m>



<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__15_Multiple_Integration__15__2_Double_Integrals_over_More_General_Regions__15__2__7__pg-image-2.png" width="34%"/>



Using Theorem 2 we obtain the following integral:



<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{D}} 50 x^{4} y \,dA \amp =\amp 
\int_0^2 \int_{y}^4 50 x^{4} y \,dx \,dy=
\int_0^2 50 \frac{x^{5} y}{5}\bigg|_{x=y}^{x=4} \,dy\\
\amp =\amp 
\int_0^2 10 y\left({4}^{5}-{y}^{5}\right) \,dy=\int_0^2 \left(10240 y-10 y^{6}\right) \,dy\\
\amp =\amp 
10240 \frac{y^2}{2}-10 \frac{y^{7}}{7}\bigg|_0^2=
\frac{10240\cdot {2}^2}{2}-\frac{10\cdot 2^{7}}{7}\\
\amp =\amp 
\frac{142080}{7} = 20297.1
\end{array}</me>



</solution>
